# cryptomine.rules — plaintext Stratum mining-protocol signatures (T1496.001).
#
# Signature companion to ../zeek/cryptomine-pool.zeek, and the WEAKER half of the pair
# by design. Read this before relying on it:
#
#   The documented attack these detections pair with (dotfiles-Kali companion entry
#   resource-hijack-xmrig) runs xmrig with `--tls`. Against that command, every rule in
#   this file matches nothing, because there is no plaintext to match. The Zeek script
#   is what covers it — it keys on the shape of the conversation (Stratum port, long
#   duration, low sustained byte rate) and never inspects payload, so TLS costs it
#   nothing.
#
# So what is this for? Plaintext Stratum is still common in the wild — commodity
# cryptojacking, misconfigured miners, and anything shipped before pools offered TLS —
# and it is cheap to match exactly. Ship it knowing it catches the lazy case, not the
# documented one. A green result here is not evidence the Zeek half works, and a silent
# result here is not evidence there is no miner.
#
# sids continue the local/custom range used by c2.rules (9_000_1xx) and coercion.rules;
# renumber if it collides with your ruleset. Alert-and-investigate, not drop — blockchain
# nodes, dev workstations, and CI runners doing legitimate crypto work all look like this.

# ── Stratum handshake (T1496.001) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The protocol's opening exchange is JSON-RPC with a small, fixed method vocabulary.
# `mining.subscribe` is the first thing a miner sends; `mining.authorize` follows with
# the wallet address. Matching the method name is exact rather than heuristic — these
# strings do not occur in ordinary traffic — which is why the plaintext case is worth
# a rule at all.
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE Cryptomining - Stratum mining.subscribe (plaintext)"; flow:to_server,established; content:"mining.subscribe"; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0040, mitre_technique_id T1496.001; sid:9000121; rev:1;)
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE Cryptomining - Stratum mining.authorize (plaintext)"; flow:to_server,established; content:"mining.authorize"; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0040, mitre_technique_id T1496.001; sid:9000122; rev:1;)

# Share submission — the steady-state message once mining is under way. Catches a
# session that was already established when capture started, which the handshake rules
# above cannot see.
alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE Cryptomining - Stratum share submission (plaintext)"; flow:to_server,established; content:"mining.submit"; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0040, mitre_technique_id T1496.001; sid:9000123; rev:1;)

# ── Known pool addresses (T1496.001) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# The indicator fast path, and the only rule here that survives TLS — it matches the
# DESTINATION, not the payload. Pool infrastructure rotates, so the addresses are NOT
# hard-coded: ../update-pool-feed.sh regenerates cryptomine-pool.lst from a maintained
# feed, one address per line; empty = empty set = matches nothing, so it cannot misfire
# on stale data. The Zeek script reads the same feed into CryptoMine::pool_hosts.
#
# Ships COMMENTED because an empty/absent dataset file fails rule load on some builds,
# and because the `load` path is deployment-specific. To enable: run
# ../update-pool-feed.sh, fix the `load` path to your cryptomine-pool.lst, and uncomment.
# alert ip $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE Cryptomining - known pool destination (maintained feed)"; dataset:isset,cryptomine-pool,type string,load cryptomine-pool.lst; classtype:trojan-activity; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0040, mitre_technique_id T1496.001; sid:9000124; rev:1;)
